<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        Turn every attachment that hangs off a lecture directly into an attachment video unit of that lecture.

        Such an attachment is one with a lecture_id but no attachment_unit_id. It used to be reachable through a section
        of the lecture that no longer exists, so without this migration its file would still sit on disk with nothing in
        the user interface pointing at it. An attachment that belongs to an attachment video unit already has
        attachment_unit_id set and is left alone.

        The new unit carries the attachment's name and release date and is appended after the units the lecture already
        has, ordered by attachment id so that two attachments of the same lecture keep their relative order. Appending
        after the highest order the lecture holds is what keeps the new units out of the way of the existing ones even
        when those are not numbered from zero and leave gaps; Lecture.updateLectureUnitOrder renumbers the whole lecture
        from zero the next time it is saved, and reads the units back in the order this changelog wrote them. The
        attachment itself is not rewritten beyond the new attachment_unit_id: it keeps its link, its version and its
        lecture_id, so the file it names keeps resolving from where it already lies, under
        uploads/attachments/lecture/{lectureId}. That is also why FileResource keeps serving
        files/attachments/lectures/{lectureId}/{name}: links to those files appear in markdown written years ago, and
        moving the files is not something a changelog can do.

        lecture_id stays populated for exactly that reason. It resolves the course for the authorization check on the
        endpoint above and it names the directory the file lives in, so it is not stale state and is not dropped here.
        Nothing else in the schema falls out of use: attachment.exercise_id still backs Exercise.attachments, and the
        lecture side of the association was a mappedBy collection rather than a table of its own.

        migrated_from_attachment_id exists only to carry the generated unit id back to the attachment row and is dropped
        again at the end of this changelog. It is indexed while it exists because the statement that reads it back is
        correlated per attachment row, and without the index that statement degrades from a fraction of a second to
        half a minute on PostgreSQL and to several minutes on MySQL for a table holding twenty thousand of these
        attachments, all of it with the attachment table locked.

        During a rolling deployment a node on the previous version keeps listing these attachments in the lecture while
        also showing the new units, and its lecture attachment section still offers to delete one, which would leave the
        unit created here without its attachment. That window closes as soon as every node is on this version.

        No endpoint on either version creates an attachment that names a lecture, with one exception: importing a
        lecture on a node of the previous version copies lecture.attachments into the new lecture, and since this
        changelog leaves lecture_id populated, the attachments of the units it created are copied that way too. A
        lecture imported on an old node during the deployment window can therefore end up with attachments that name a
        lecture and no unit, which the new version does not show. They are found with

            SELECT id FROM attachment WHERE lecture_id IS NOT NULL AND attachment_unit_id IS NULL

        which returns nothing once every node is on this version, since no code path left on this version writes such a
        row. A later changelog can convert whatever that query returns the same way this one does.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260905235721-01-add-migration-helper-column" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="lecture_unit" columnName="migrated_from_attachment_id"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            Temporary column that maps a created lecture unit back to the attachment it was created for.

            The precondition is what makes this changeset safe to run again. Adding a column commits on its own on
            MySQL, so a run that is killed between the statement and the databasechangelog row leaves the column behind
            without a record of it, and an unguarded addColumn would then fail on every following start and keep the
            whole application down.
        </comment>
        <addColumn tableName="lecture_unit">
            <column name="migrated_from_attachment_id" type="bigint"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260905235721-01b-index-migration-helper-column" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <indexExists tableName="lecture_unit" indexName="lecture_unit_migrated_from_attachment"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Index the helper column, so that reading it back per attachment row is a lookup rather than a scan.</comment>
        <createIndex tableName="lecture_unit" indexName="lecture_unit_migrated_from_attachment">
            <column name="migrated_from_attachment_id"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260905235721-02-create-units-for-lecture-attachments" author="krusche">
        <comment>Create one attachment video unit per attachment that is attached to a lecture directly.</comment>
        <sql>
            INSERT INTO lecture_unit (discriminator, name, release_date, lecture_id, lecture_unit_order, migrated_from_attachment_id)
            SELECT 'A',
                   attachment.name,
                   attachment.release_date,
                   attachment.lecture_id,
                   COALESCE(existing.highest_order, -1) + ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY attachment.lecture_id ORDER BY attachment.id),
                   attachment.id
            FROM attachment
                LEFT JOIN (SELECT lecture_id, MAX(lecture_unit_order) AS highest_order FROM lecture_unit GROUP BY lecture_id) existing
                    ON existing.lecture_id = attachment.lecture_id
            WHERE attachment.lecture_id IS NOT NULL
              AND attachment.attachment_unit_id IS NULL
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!--
                Deleting a unit takes its generated state with it: the ingestion state, the transcription and the Iris
                sync row are removed by their own cascades and a slide that names the unit loses that reference. All of
                it is derived from the attachment and is produced again once the unit is created a second time. Which
                units this deletes is decided in the rollback of the last changeset, which is what fills the column
                again; when it runs there, the units that carry something a rollback must not destroy are left out.
            -->
            <sql>DELETE FROM lecture_unit WHERE migrated_from_attachment_id IS NOT NULL</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260905235721-03-attach-migrated-attachments-to-units" author="krusche">
        <comment>Point each migrated attachment at the lecture unit that was created for it.</comment>
        <sql>
            UPDATE attachment
            SET attachment_unit_id = (SELECT unit.id FROM lecture_unit unit WHERE unit.migrated_from_attachment_id = attachment.id)
            WHERE attachment.attachment_unit_id IS NULL
              AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lecture_unit unit WHERE unit.migrated_from_attachment_id = attachment.id)
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>
                UPDATE attachment
                SET attachment_unit_id = NULL
                WHERE attachment_unit_id IN (SELECT unit.id FROM lecture_unit unit WHERE unit.migrated_from_attachment_id IS NOT NULL)
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260905235721-04-drop-migration-helper-column" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <columnExists tableName="lecture_unit" columnName="migrated_from_attachment_id"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            Drop the temporary column now that every migrated attachment names its unit. Dropping a column commits on
            its own on MySQL, so the precondition is here for the same reason as the one on the changeset that added it.
        </comment>
        <dropColumn tableName="lecture_unit" columnName="migrated_from_attachment_id"/>
        <rollback>
            <addColumn tableName="lecture_unit">
                <column name="migrated_from_attachment_id" type="bigint"/>
            </addColumn>
            <createIndex tableName="lecture_unit" indexName="lecture_unit_migrated_from_attachment">
                <column name="migrated_from_attachment_id"/>
            </createIndex>
            <!--
                Recover the mapping from what the rows themselves still say. A unit this changelog created is the only
                kind whose attachment names both the unit and the unit's own lecture: an attachment that was uploaded
                into a unit never carried a lecture id, and the copy that importing a lecture makes carries the id of
                the unit in its link but no lecture id either. The link is only used to tell whether the file is still
                the one the migration left in place; a unit whose file was replaced through the unit editor names the
                unit directory instead and is left alone, because its file has moved there and deleting the unit would
                strand it. An attachment with no link at all still counts, since nothing has moved its file.

                Units that carry something a rollback must not destroy are left out: a competency link and a completion
                are the two references to a lecture unit the schema refuses to delete, so leaving those units in place
                is what keeps the rollback of the previous changesets from failing halfway and leaving the database
                between the two states. Their attachments stay attached, which is the same position a rolling
                deployment leaves them in, and is the one this changelog is designed around.

                The subquery takes the lowest id rather than the only one, because nothing in the schema stops two
                attachments from naming the same unit, and a rollback that fails on a row like that would be worse than
                one that picks the first.
            -->
            <sql>
                UPDATE lecture_unit
                SET migrated_from_attachment_id = (SELECT MIN(attachment.id)
                                                   FROM attachment
                                                   WHERE attachment.attachment_unit_id = lecture_unit.id
                                                     AND attachment.lecture_id = lecture_unit.lecture_id
                                                     AND (attachment.jhi_link IS NULL OR attachment.jhi_link LIKE 'attachments/lecture/%'))
                WHERE lecture_unit.discriminator = 'A'
                  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM competency_lecture_unit link WHERE link.lecture_unit_id = lecture_unit.id)
                  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lecture_unit_user completion WHERE completion.lecture_unit_id = lecture_unit.id)
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
